[1mNAME[0m
       elf - format of Executable and Linking Format (ELF) files

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1m#include <elf.h>[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       The  header  file  [4m<elf.h>[24m  defines  the format of ELF executable binary files.  Amongst these files are normal executable files, relocatable object
       files, core files, and shared objects.

       An executable file using the ELF file format consists of an ELF header, followed by a program header table or a section header table, or both.   The
       ELF  header is always at offset zero of the file.  The program header table and the section header table's offset in the file are defined in the ELF
       header.  The two tables describe the rest of the particularities of the file.

       This header file describes the above mentioned headers as C structures and also includes structures for dynamic sections,  relocation  sections  and
       symbol tables.

   [1mBasic types[0m
       The following types are used for N-bit architectures (N=32,64, [4mElfN[24m stands for [4mElf32[24m or [4mElf64[24m, [4muintN_t[24m stands for [4muint32_t[24m or [4muint64_t[24m):

           ElfN_Addr       Unsigned program address, uintN_t
           ElfN_Off        Unsigned file offset, uintN_t
           ElfN_Section    Unsigned section index, uint16_t
           ElfN_Versym     Unsigned version symbol information, uint16_t
           Elf_Byte        unsigned char
           ElfN_Half       uint16_t
           ElfN_Sword      int32_t
           ElfN_Word       uint32_t
           ElfN_Sxword     int64_t
           ElfN_Xword      uint64_t

       (Note: the *BSD terminology is a bit different.  There, [4mElf64_Half[24m is twice as large as [4mElf32_Half[24m, and [4mElf64Quarter[24m is used for [4muint16_t[24m.  In order
       to avoid confusion these types are replaced by explicit ones in the below.)

       All  data  structures  that  the  file format defines follow the "natural" size and alignment guidelines for the relevant class.  If necessary, data
       structures contain explicit padding to ensure 4-byte alignment for 4-byte objects, to force structure sizes to a multiple of 4, and so on.

   [1mELF header (Ehdr)[0m
       The ELF header is described by the type [4mElf32_Ehdr[24m or [4mElf64_Ehdr[24m:

           #define EI_NIDENT 16

           typedef struct {
               unsigned char e_ident[EI_NIDENT];
               uint16_t      e_type;
               uint16_t      e_machine;
               uint32_t      e_version;
               ElfN_Addr     e_entry;
               ElfN_Off      e_phoff;
               ElfN_Off      e_shoff;
               uint32_t      e_flags;
               uint16_t      e_ehsize;
               uint16_t      e_phentsize;
               uint16_t      e_phnum;
               uint16_t      e_shentsize;
               uint16_t      e_shnum;
               uint16_t      e_shstrndx;
           } ElfN_Ehdr;

       The fields have the following meanings:

       [4me_ident[0m
              This array of bytes specifies how to interpret the file, independent of the processor or the file's remaining contents.   Within  this  array
              everything  is named by macros, which start with the prefix [1mEI_ [22mand may contain values which start with the prefix [1mELF[22m.  The following macros
              are defined:

              [1mEI_MAG0[0m
                     The first byte of the magic number.  It must be filled with [1mELFMAG0[22m.  (0: 0x7f)

              [1mEI_MAG1[0m
                     The second byte of the magic number.  It must be filled with [1mELFMAG1[22m.  (1: 'E')

              [1mEI_MAG2[0m
                     The third byte of the magic number.  It must be filled with [1mELFMAG2[22m.  (2: 'L')

              [1mEI_MAG3[0m
                     The fourth byte of the magic number.  It must be filled with [1mELFMAG3[22m.  (3: 'F')

              [1mEI_CLASS[0m
                     The fifth byte identifies the architecture for this binary:

                     [1mELFCLASSNONE  [22mThis class is invalid.
                     [1mELFCLASS32    [22mThis defines the 32-bit architecture.  It supports machines with files and virtual address spaces up to 4 Gigabytes.
                     [1mELFCLASS64    [22mThis defines the 64-bit architecture.

              [1mEI_DATA[0m
                     The sixth byte specifies the data encoding of the processor-specific data in the file.  Currently, these encodings are supported:

                       [1mELFDATANONE   [22mUnknown data format.
                       [1mELFDATA2LSB   [22mTwo's complement, little-endian.
                       [1mELFDATA2MSB   [22mTwo's complement, big-endian.

              [1mEI_VERSION[0m
                     The seventh byte is the version number of the ELF specification:

                     [1mEV_NONE       [22mInvalid version.
                     [1mEV_CURRENT    [22mCurrent version.

              [1mEI_OSABI[0m
                     The eighth byte identifies the operating system and ABI to which the object is targeted.  Some fields in  other  ELF  structures  have
                     flags  and  values  that  have platform-specific meanings; the interpretation of those fields is determined by the value of this byte.
                     For example:

                     [1mELFOSABI_NONE        [22mSame as ELFOSABI_SYSV
                     [1mELFOSABI_SYSV        [22mUNIX System V ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_HPUX        [22mHP-UX ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_NETBSD      [22mNetBSD ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_LINUX       [22mLinux ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_SOLARIS     [22mSolaris ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_IRIX        [22mIRIX ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_FREEBSD     [22mFreeBSD ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_TRU64       [22mTRU64 UNIX ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_ARM         [22mARM architecture ABI
                     [1mELFOSABI_STANDALONE  [22mStand-alone (embedded) ABI

              [1mEI_ABIVERSION[0m
                     The ninth byte identifies the version of the ABI to which the object is targeted.  This field is used to distinguish among  incompati‐
                     ble versions of an ABI.  The interpretation of this version number is dependent on the ABI identified by the [1mEI_OSABI [22mfield.  Applica‐
                     tions conforming to this specification use the value 0.

              [1mEI_PAD [22mStart  of padding.  These bytes are reserved and set to zero.  Programs which read them should ignore them.  The value for [1mEI_PAD [22mwill
                     change in the future if currently unused bytes are given meanings.

              [1mEI_NIDENT[0m
                     The size of the [4me_ident[24m array.

       [4me_type[24m This member of the structure identifies the object file type:

              [1mET_NONE         [22mAn unknown type.
              [1mET_REL          [22mA relocatable file.
              [1mET_EXEC         [22mAn executable file.
              [1mET_DYN          [22mA shared object.
              [1mET_CORE         [22mA core file.

       [4me_machine[0m
              This member specifies the required architecture for an individual file.  For example:

              [1mEM_NONE         [22mAn unknown machine
              [1mEM_M32          [22mAT&T WE 32100
              [1mEM_SPARC        [22mSun Microsystems SPARC
              [1mEM_386          [22mIntel 80386
              [1mEM_68K          [22mMotorola 68000
              [1mEM_88K          [22mMotorola 88000
              [1mEM_860          [22mIntel 80860
              [1mEM_MIPS         [22mMIPS RS3000 (big-endian only)
              [1mEM_PARISC       [22mHP/PA
              [1mEM_SPARC32PLUS  [22mSPARC with enhanced instruction set
              [1mEM_PPC          [22mPowerPC
              [1mEM_PPC64        [22mPowerPC 64-bit
              [1mEM_S390         [22mIBM S/390
              [1mEM_ARM          [22mAdvanced RISC Machines
              [1mEM_SH           [22mRenesas SuperH
              [1mEM_SPARCV9      [22mSPARC v9 64-bit
              [1mEM_IA_64        [22mIntel Itanium
              [1mEM_X86_64       [22mAMD x86-64
              [1mEM_VAX          [22mDEC Vax

       [4me_version[0m
              This member identifies the file version:

              [1mEV_NONE         [22mInvalid version
              [1mEV_CURRENT      [22mCurrent version

       [4me_entry[0m
              This member gives the virtual address to which the system first transfers control, thus starting the process.  If the file has no  associated
              entry point, this member holds zero.

       [4me_phoff[0m
              This member holds the program header table's file offset in bytes.  If the file has no program header table, this member holds zero.

       [4me_shoff[0m
              This member holds the section header table's file offset in bytes.  If the file has no section header table, this member holds zero.

       [4me_flags[0m
              This  member  holds processor-specific flags associated with the file.  Flag names take the form EF_`machine_flag'.  Currently, no flags have
              been defined.

       [4me_ehsize[0m
              This member holds the ELF header's size in bytes.

       [4me_phentsize[0m
              This member holds the size in bytes of one entry in the file's program header table; all entries are the same size.

       [4me_phnum[0m
              This member holds the number of entries in the program header table.  Thus the product of [4me_phentsize[24m and [4me_phnum[24m gives the table's  size  in
              bytes.  If a file has no program header, [4me_phnum[24m holds the value zero.

              If  the number of entries in the program header table is larger than or equal to [1mPN_XNUM [22m(0xffff), this member holds [1mPN_XNUM [22m(0xffff) and the
              real number of entries in the program header table is held in the [4msh_info[24m member of the initial entry in section  header  table.   Otherwise,
              the [4msh_info[24m member of the initial entry contains the value zero.

              [1mPN_XNUM[0m
                     This is defined as 0xffff, the largest number [4me_phnum[24m can have, specifying where the actual number of program headers is assigned.

       [4me_shentsize[0m
              This  member  holds  a  sections header's size in bytes.  A section header is one entry in the section header table; all entries are the same
              size.

       [4me_shnum[0m
              This member holds the number of entries in the section header table.  Thus the product of [4me_shentsize[24m and [4me_shnum[24m gives  the  section  header
              table's size in bytes.  If a file has no section header table, [4me_shnum[24m holds the value of zero.

              If  the number of entries in the section header table is larger than or equal to [1mSHN_LORESERVE [22m(0xff00), [4me_shnum[24m holds the value zero and the
              real number of entries in the section header table is held in the [4msh_size[24m member of the initial entry in section  header  table.   Otherwise,
              the [4msh_size[24m member of the initial entry in the section header table holds the value zero.

       [4me_shstrndx[0m
              This member holds the section header table index of the entry associated with the section name string table.  If the file has no section name
              string table, this member holds the value [1mSHN_UNDEF[22m.

              If  the  index  of section name string table section is larger than or equal to [1mSHN_LORESERVE [22m(0xff00), this member holds [1mSHN_XINDEX [22m(0xffff)
              and the real index of the section name string table section is held in the [4msh_link[24m member of the initial entry in section header table.  Oth‐
              erwise, the [4msh_link[24m member of the initial entry in section header table contains the value zero.

   [1mProgram header (Phdr)[0m
       An executable or shared object file's program header table is an array of structures, each describing a segment  or  other  information  the  system
       needs  to  prepare  the  program for execution.  An object file [4msegment[24m contains one or more [4msections[24m.  Program headers are meaningful only for exe‐
       cutable and shared object files.  A file specifies its own program header size with the ELF header's [4me_phentsize[24m and [4me_phnum[24m members.  The ELF  pro‐
       gram header is described by the type [4mElf32_Phdr[24m or [4mElf64_Phdr[24m depending on the architecture:

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t   p_type;
               Elf32_Off  p_offset;
               Elf32_Addr p_vaddr;
               Elf32_Addr p_paddr;
               uint32_t   p_filesz;
               uint32_t   p_memsz;
               uint32_t   p_flags;
               uint32_t   p_align;
           } Elf32_Phdr;

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t   p_type;
               uint32_t   p_flags;
               Elf64_Off  p_offset;
               Elf64_Addr p_vaddr;
               Elf64_Addr p_paddr;
               uint64_t   p_filesz;
               uint64_t   p_memsz;
               uint64_t   p_align;
           } Elf64_Phdr;

       The main difference between the 32-bit and the 64-bit program header lies in the location of the [4mp_flags[24m member in the total struct.

       [4mp_type[24m This member of the structure indicates what kind of segment this array element describes or how to interpret the array element's information.

                 [1mPT_NULL[0m
                        The array element is unused and the other members' values are undefined.  This lets the program header have ignored entries.

                 [1mPT_LOAD[0m
                        The  array  element specifies a loadable segment, described by [4mp_filesz[24m and [4mp_memsz[24m.  The bytes from the file are mapped to the be‐
                        ginning of the memory segment.  If the segment's memory size [4mp_memsz[24m is larger than the file size [4mp_filesz[24m, the "extra"  bytes  are
                        defined  to  hold  the value 0 and to follow the segment's initialized area.  The file size may not be larger than the memory size.
                        Loadable segment entries in the program header table appear in ascending order, sorted on the [4mp_vaddr[24m member.

                 [1mPT_DYNAMIC[0m
                        The array element specifies dynamic linking information.

                 [1mPT_INTERP[0m
                        The array element specifies the location and size of a null-terminated pathname to invoke as an interpreter.  This segment type  is
                        meaningful  only for executable files (though it may occur for shared objects).  However it may not occur more than once in a file.
                        If it is present, it must precede any loadable segment entry.

                 [1mPT_NOTE[0m
                        The array element specifies the location of notes (ElfN_Nhdr).

                 [1mPT_SHLIB[0m
                        This segment type is reserved but has unspecified semantics.  Programs that contain an array element of this type do not conform to
                        the ABI.

                 [1mPT_PHDR[0m
                        The array element, if present, specifies the location and size of the program header table itself, both in the file and in the mem‐
                        ory image of the program.  This segment type may not occur more than once in a file.  Moreover, it may occur only  if  the  program
                        header table is part of the memory image of the program.  If it is present, it must precede any loadable segment entry.

                 [1mPT_LOPROC[0m
                 [1mPT_HIPROC[0m
                        Values in the inclusive range [[1mPT_LOPROC[22m, [1mPT_HIPROC[22m] are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

                 [1mPT_GNU_STACK[0m
                        GNU extension which is used by the Linux kernel to control the state of the stack via the flags set in the [4mp_flags[24m member.

       [4mp_offset[0m
              This member holds the offset from the beginning of the file at which the first byte of the segment resides.

       [4mp_vaddr[0m
              This member holds the virtual address at which the first byte of the segment resides in memory.

       [4mp_paddr[0m
              On  systems  for which physical addressing is relevant, this member is reserved for the segment's physical address.  Under BSD this member is
              not used and must be zero.

       [4mp_filesz[0m
              This member holds the number of bytes in the file image of the segment.  It may be zero.

       [4mp_memsz[0m
              This member holds the number of bytes in the memory image of the segment.  It may be zero.

       [4mp_flags[0m
              This member holds a bit mask of flags relevant to the segment:

              [1mPF_X   [22mAn executable segment.
              [1mPF_W   [22mA writable segment.
              [1mPF_R   [22mA readable segment.

              A text segment commonly has the flags [1mPF_X [22mand [1mPF_R[22m.  A data segment commonly has [1mPF_W [22mand [1mPF_R[22m.

       [4mp_align[0m
              This member holds the value to which the segments are aligned in memory and in the file.  Loadable process segments must have congruent  val‐
              ues  for  [4mp_vaddr[24m  and [4mp_offset[24m, modulo the page size.  Values of zero and one mean no alignment is required.  Otherwise, [4mp_align[24m should be a
              positive, integral power of two, and [4mp_vaddr[24m should equal [4mp_offset[24m, modulo [4mp_align[24m.

   [1mSection header (Shdr)[0m
       A file's section header table lets one locate all the file's sections.  The section header table is an array of [4mElf32_Shdr[24m or [4mElf64_Shdr[24m structures.
       The ELF header's [4me_shoff[24m member gives the byte offset from the beginning of the file to the section header table.  [4me_shnum[24m holds the number  of  en‐
       tries the section header table contains.  [4me_shentsize[24m holds the size in bytes of each entry.

       A  section header table index is a subscript into this array.  Some section header table indices are reserved: the initial entry and the indices be‐
       tween [1mSHN_LORESERVE [22mand [1mSHN_HIRESERVE[22m.  The initial entry is used in ELF extensions for [4me_phnum[24m, [4me_shnum[24m, and [4me_shstrndx[24m; in other cases, each field
       in the initial entry is set to zero.  An object file does not have sections for these special indices:

       [1mSHN_UNDEF[0m
              This value marks an undefined, missing, irrelevant, or otherwise meaningless section reference.

       [1mSHN_LORESERVE[0m
              This value specifies the lower bound of the range of reserved indices.

       [1mSHN_LOPROC[0m
       [1mSHN_HIPROC[0m
              Values greater in the inclusive range [[1mSHN_LOPROC[22m, [1mSHN_HIPROC[22m] are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

       [1mSHN_ABS[0m
              This value specifies the absolute value for the corresponding reference.  For example, a symbol defined relative to  section  number  [1mSHN_ABS[0m
              has an absolute value and is not affected by relocation.

       [1mSHN_COMMON[0m
              Symbols defined relative to this section are common symbols, such as FORTRAN COMMON or unallocated C external variables.

       [1mSHN_HIRESERVE[0m
              This  value specifies the upper bound of the range of reserved indices.  The system reserves indices between [1mSHN_LORESERVE [22mand [1mSHN_HIRESERVE[22m,
              inclusive.  The section header table does not contain entries for the reserved indices.

       The section header has the following structure:

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t   sh_name;
               uint32_t   sh_type;
               uint32_t   sh_flags;
               Elf32_Addr sh_addr;
               Elf32_Off  sh_offset;
               uint32_t   sh_size;
               uint32_t   sh_link;
               uint32_t   sh_info;
               uint32_t   sh_addralign;
               uint32_t   sh_entsize;
           } Elf32_Shdr;

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t   sh_name;
               uint32_t   sh_type;
               uint64_t   sh_flags;
               Elf64_Addr sh_addr;
               Elf64_Off  sh_offset;
               uint64_t   sh_size;
               uint32_t   sh_link;
               uint32_t   sh_info;
               uint64_t   sh_addralign;
               uint64_t   sh_entsize;
           } Elf64_Shdr;

       No real differences exist between the 32-bit and 64-bit section headers.

       [4msh_name[0m
              This member specifies the name of the section.  Its value is an index into the section header string table section, giving the location of  a
              null-terminated string.

       [4msh_type[0m
              This member categorizes the section's contents and semantics.

              [1mSHT_NULL[0m
                     This  value  marks  the section header as inactive.  It does not have an associated section.  Other members of the section header have
                     undefined values.

              [1mSHT_PROGBITS[0m
                     This section holds information defined by the program, whose format and meaning are determined solely by the program.

              [1mSHT_SYMTAB[0m
                     This section holds a symbol table.  Typically, [1mSHT_SYMTAB [22mprovides symbols for link editing, though it may also be  used  for  dynamic
                     linking.   As a complete symbol table, it may contain many symbols unnecessary for dynamic linking.  An object file can also contain a
                     [1mSHT_DYNSYM [22msection.

              [1mSHT_STRTAB[0m
                     This section holds a string table.  An object file may have multiple string table sections.

              [1mSHT_RELA[0m
                     This section holds relocation entries with explicit addends, such as type [4mElf32_Rela[24m for the 32-bit class of object files.  An  object
                     may have multiple relocation sections.

              [1mSHT_HASH[0m
                     This  section holds a symbol hash table.  An object participating in dynamic linking must contain a symbol hash table.  An object file
                     may have only one hash table.

              [1mSHT_DYNAMIC[0m
                     This section holds information for dynamic linking.  An object file may have only one dynamic section.

              [1mSHT_NOTE[0m
                     This section holds notes (ElfN_Nhdr).

              [1mSHT_NOBITS[0m
                     A section of this type occupies no space in the file but otherwise resembles [1mSHT_PROGBITS[22m.  Although this section contains  no  bytes,
                     the [4msh_offset[24m member contains the conceptual file offset.

              [1mSHT_REL[0m
                     This  section  holds relocation offsets without explicit addends, such as type [4mElf32_Rel[24m for the 32-bit class of object files.  An ob‐
                     ject file may have multiple relocation sections.

              [1mSHT_SHLIB[0m
                     This section is reserved but has unspecified semantics.

              [1mSHT_DYNSYM[0m
                     This section holds a minimal set of dynamic linking symbols.  An object file can also contain a [1mSHT_SYMTAB [22msection.

              [1mSHT_LOPROC[0m
              [1mSHT_HIPROC[0m
                     Values in the inclusive range [[1mSHT_LOPROC[22m, [1mSHT_HIPROC[22m] are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

              [1mSHT_LOUSER[0m
                     This value specifies the lower bound of the range of indices reserved for application programs.

              [1mSHT_HIUSER[0m
                     This value specifies the upper bound of the range of indices reserved for application programs.  Section types between [1mSHT_LOUSER  [22mand
                     [1mSHT_HIUSER [22mmay be used by the application, without conflicting with current or future system-defined section types.

       [4msh_flags[0m
              Sections  support one-bit flags that describe miscellaneous attributes.  If a flag bit is set in [4msh_flags[24m, the attribute is "on" for the sec‐
              tion.  Otherwise, the attribute is "off" or does not apply.  Undefined attributes are set to zero.

              [1mSHF_WRITE[0m
                     This section contains data that should be writable during process execution.

              [1mSHF_ALLOC[0m
                     This section occupies memory during process execution.  Some control sections do not reside in the memory image  of  an  object  file.
                     This attribute is off for those sections.

              [1mSHF_EXECINSTR[0m
                     This section contains executable machine instructions.

              [1mSHF_MASKPROC[0m
                     All bits included in this mask are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

       [4msh_addr[0m
              If  this  section  appears  in  the memory image of a process, this member holds the address at which the section's first byte should reside.
              Otherwise, the member contains zero.

       [4msh_offset[0m
              This member's value holds the byte offset from the beginning of the file to the first byte in the section.  One section type, [1mSHT_NOBITS[22m, oc‐
              cupies no space in the file, and its [4msh_offset[24m member locates the conceptual placement in the file.

       [4msh_size[0m
              This member holds the section's size in bytes.  Unless the section type is [1mSHT_NOBITS[22m, the section occupies [4msh_size[24m bytes  in  the  file.   A
              section of type [1mSHT_NOBITS [22mmay have a nonzero size, but it occupies no space in the file.

       [4msh_link[0m
              This member holds a section header table index link, whose interpretation depends on the section type.

       [4msh_info[0m
              This member holds extra information, whose interpretation depends on the section type.

       [4msh_addralign[0m
              Some  sections  have address alignment constraints.  If a section holds a doubleword, the system must ensure doubleword alignment for the en‐
              tire section.  That is, the value of [4msh_addr[24m must be congruent to zero, modulo the value of [4msh_addralign[24m.  Only zero  and  positive  integral
              powers of two are allowed.  The value 0 or 1 means that the section has no alignment constraints.

       [4msh_entsize[0m
              Some  sections hold a table of fixed-sized entries, such as a symbol table.  For such a section, this member gives the size in bytes for each
              entry.  This member contains zero if the section does not hold a table of fixed-size entries.

       Various sections hold program and control information:

       [4m.bss[24m   This section holds uninitialized data that contributes to the program's memory image.  By definition, the system initializes  the  data  with
              zeros when the program begins to run.  This section is of type [1mSHT_NOBITS[22m.  The attribute types are [1mSHF_ALLOC [22mand [1mSHF_WRITE[22m.

       [4m.comment[0m
              This section holds version control information.  This section is of type [1mSHT_PROGBITS[22m.  No attribute types are used.

