   [1mString and symbol tables[0m
       String table sections hold null-terminated character sequences, commonly called strings.  The object file uses these strings to represent symbol and
       section names.  One references a string as an index into the string table section.  The first byte, which is index zero, is defined to hold  a  null
       byte ('\0').  Similarly, a string table's last byte is defined to hold a null byte, ensuring null termination for all strings.

       An object file's symbol table holds information needed to locate and relocate a program's symbolic definitions and references.  A symbol table index
       is a subscript into this array.

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t      st_name;
               Elf32_Addr    st_value;
               uint32_t      st_size;
               unsigned char st_info;
               unsigned char st_other;
               uint16_t      st_shndx;
           } Elf32_Sym;

           typedef struct {
               uint32_t      st_name;
               unsigned char st_info;
               unsigned char st_other;
               uint16_t      st_shndx;
               Elf64_Addr    st_value;
               uint64_t      st_size;
           } Elf64_Sym;

       The 32-bit and 64-bit versions have the same members, just in a different order.

       [4mst_name[0m
              This  member  holds  an  index into the object file's symbol string table, which holds character representations of the symbol names.  If the
              value is nonzero, it represents a string table index that gives the symbol name.  Otherwise, the symbol has no name.

       [4mst_value[0m
              This member gives the value of the associated symbol.

       [4mst_size[0m
              Many symbols have associated sizes.  This member holds zero if the symbol has no size or an unknown size.

       [4mst_info[0m
              This member specifies the symbol's type and binding attributes:

              [1mSTT_NOTYPE[0m
                     The symbol's type is not defined.

              [1mSTT_OBJECT[0m
                     The symbol is associated with a data object.

              [1mSTT_FUNC[0m
                     The symbol is associated with a function or other executable code.

              [1mSTT_SECTION[0m
                     The symbol is associated with a section.  Symbol table entries of this type exist primarily for relocation and normally have [1mSTB_LOCAL[0m
                     bindings.

              [1mSTT_FILE[0m
                     By convention, the symbol's name gives the name of the source file associated with the object file.  A file symbol has [1mSTB_LOCAL [22mbind‐
                     ings, its section index is [1mSHN_ABS[22m, and it precedes the other [1mSTB_LOCAL [22msymbols of the file, if it is present.

              [1mSTT_LOPROC[0m
              [1mSTT_HIPROC[0m
                     Values in the inclusive range [[1mSTT_LOPROC[22m, [1mSTT_HIPROC[22m] are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

              [1mSTB_LOCAL[0m
                     Local symbols are not visible outside the object file containing their definition.  Local symbols of the same name may exist in multi‐
                     ple files without interfering with each other.

              [1mSTB_GLOBAL[0m
                     Global symbols are visible to all object files being combined.  One file's definition of a global symbol will satisfy  another  file's
                     undefined reference to the same symbol.

              [1mSTB_WEAK[0m
                     Weak symbols resemble global symbols, but their definitions have lower precedence.

              [1mSTB_LOPROC[0m
              [1mSTB_HIPROC[0m
                     Values in the inclusive range [[1mSTB_LOPROC[22m, [1mSTB_HIPROC[22m] are reserved for processor-specific semantics.

